prompt:
A photorealistic macro still-life of a string of fairy lights, lying loose on a perfectly flat, even 50% mid-grey surface, exact colour HEX #808080, edge to edge — no texture, no gradient, no vignette, no horizon. Nothing else in the picture. Shot from above and slightly to one side, the string filling most of the frame.

THE OBJECT IS THE GARLAND FROM <<<image_1>>>, TAKEN OFF THE SCOOTER AND LAID DOWN ON ITS OWN: the same bulbs in the same shape, size, colour and spacing, the same wire, the same fittings, the same wear. Nothing redesigned, nothing added, nothing removed. Only the scooter is gone.

IT IS A CLOSED LOOP: the two ends of the string are joined together, so the whole thing is one continuous ring of cable with no loose end anywhere in the frame. The join is visible — a small connector, or the ends twisted together and bound with a turn of tape. The loop lies collapsed on itself in slack irregular coils, folding over and crossing itself several times, never a neat circle, never a tidy ring, never symmetrical, never laid out flat and even.

IT IS SWITCHED ON: every bulb is lit, glowing a warm incandescent yellow, each throwing a small soft pool of light onto the grey surface beneath it and a gentle halation around itself. Bright enough to read as lit, never blinding, never blown out, never a hard flare. Where the cable folds over itself the pools overlap and brighten. A couple of bulbs are noticeably dimmer than the rest, and one is dark and dead in its socket.

THE HARDWARE IS REAL, AND IT SHOWS AT THIS MAGNIFICATION:
— each bulb sits in a moulded plastic socket with a visible parting line from the mould and a small burr of flash along it, the socket slightly yellowed with age;
— the glass envelope of each bulb is not perfectly smooth — a faint seam runs up its side and a small pip is left on the crown where it was sealed;
— inside, two fine support wires rise from the base and hold a tiny coiled filament, clearly visible and glowing;
— the bulbs push into their sockets and one sits crooked, half turned in its housing;
— the wire is thin twin-core cable, the two conductors twisted round each other along their whole length, its insulation slightly chalky and scuffed, kinked and springy where it was wound tightly around tubing, nicked in a couple of places where it rubbed on metal;
— two small cable ties remain fastened around the cable, one snipped short with a sharp cut end;
— a strip of old electrical tape is wound round one section as a repair, its edge peeling and dirty.

WEAR, NOT FILTH: fine road grime in the seams of the sockets, dust in the twist of the cable, a slight dullness to the plastic. Used for years, but not disgusting.

LIGHT: soft, even and ordinary ambient light from above and slightly in front, so the object is fully readable, plus its own warm glow from the bulbs. Gentle contact shadows underneath the cable and each socket, so the string sits on the surface convincingly. No dramatic key, no rim light, no coloured light, no glamour.

Ultra-photorealistic, shot on a real camera, 100mm macro at f5.6, sharp across the nearest bulbs so their filaments and mould seams are legible, real optical micro-contrast, fine natural film grain, unretouched. Muted natural colour apart from the warm glow.

AVOID: a loose end, a cut end, a plug, a battery pack, a mains adaptor, an open string, an unjoined string, a neat circle, a perfect ring, a tidy coil, a symmetrical layout, the string laid out straight, unlit bulbs, dark bulbs, cold glass, a scooter, a bike, any vehicle, a wheel, a handlebar, the garland still wrapped around something, redesigned bulbs, different bulb shapes or colours, LED pinpoints, seed lights, glowing beads with no visible filament, smooth featureless sockets, a clean new string, perfect plastic, identical flawless bulbs, a hand, fingers, a table, wood, fabric, a floor, a background object, rubble, dust on the ground, a lit or gradient background, a coloured background, dramatic lighting, high contrast, 3d render, cgi, sharpening, vignette, watermarks.
